      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your point about market incentives is valid, as companies will almost universally seek efficiency whenever profitable technology emerges. However, framing this as purely an unstoppable force ignores how policy can fundamentally alter the *cost-benefit analysis* for automation by taxing monopolistic profits or mandating human-centric AI algorithms. Furthermore, historical precedents show that rules can redirect technological application, turning a tool designed for pure replacement into one focused on augmentation or collaboration. The outcome depends less on whether robots can exist and more on whether government and unions succeed in making human labor indispensable, safer, and economically viable.",
